On Monday, August 29, 2011 9:46:29 PM UTC-4, derekt75 wrote:
> The wiki says that the knight is perhaps the easiest class in the
> game, 

I think it's one of the easier roles for beginners, but not 
as easy as Valkyrie.  (This is in vanilla.  Slash'em is a 
different matter for another thread.)

Notable differences:
 * Knights are better at riding, can get skill in polearms,
   and start with a lance.  These are advantages for experienced 
   players, but they are quite difficult skills to learn and so 
   not very helpful for beginners.  Ditto the starting saddle.
 * The Knight's starting pet is vegetarian, which makes it quite
   a bit harder to train and keep alive.  Admittedly, this
   only matters until you find tripe and a large dog.
 * Knights start with more food.  (Individually, the apples
   and carrots aren't worth much; but you get 20-40 of them.)
 * Valkyries start with higher strength.  This is a big deal.
   Actually, Valkyrie starting attributes are better than 
   Knight across the board, except for Wisdom and Charisma.  
   Wisdom is by far the easiest attribute to exercise, and 
   charisma is by far the least important.
 * Knights start with gloves.  This is minor, because in
   most games you find gloves before seeing any c.  But
   there's a nonzero chance that it will matter.
 * Knights can always jump.  This may be their most
   underrated advantage.
 * Knights are better at spellcasting.  This advantage 
   is somewhat undercut by having Int instead of Wis as
   the spell stat.  This usually means you need to
   wait for alchemy to cast mid-level spells.
   Then again, Knights are more likely to be able to read
   the books in the first place, without lenses.  On the
   whole, knights have an advantage here, but it doesn't
   really become significant until mid-game.
 * Valkyries start with basic skill in dagger.  This
   is mostly minor (basic skill does not require that
   much training), but daggers *are* very useful.  Also,
   the higher strength means a Valk is more likely to be
   able to carry a stack of daggers in the early game.
 * The Valkyrie quest artifact supplies more unusual
   extrinsics when carried but is so heavy that many
   players leave it behind.  (This is controversial,
   of course.  Many players consider it well worth
   its weight, and some will even arti-wish for it.)
   The knight artifact is MUCH lighter and supplies 
   extrinsics that you *need*, but which could be
   obtained from other sources.
 * Valks can be neutral, but I do not consider this
   a meaningful advantage for beginners, because the
   lawful alignment is easier.
 * Knights have the whole caitiff thing going on.
   Some players find this very annoying.


